# ============================================================
# CONNECT — frontend environment
# Copy to `.env.local`.
#
# There are two modes. Pick the one that matches how you are
# serving the app, because the difference decides whether the
# camera and microphone work at all.
# ============================================================

# ============================================================
# MODE A — behind the TLS terminator (`../deploy/`)   USE THIS
# ============================================================
# Required for calling. Browsers only expose `navigator.mediaDevices`
# in a secure context, and `localhost` is the one non-TLS origin
# browsers treat as secure. A plain-HTTP LAN address is not one, so
# on `http://192.168.1.46:3000` the camera and microphone APIs do
# not exist and the call buttons stay disabled.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=/api
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:4000
#
# A leading slash tells `lib/api.ts` to make every request relative to
# the page instead of pointing at an absolute API origin. That matters
# for two separate reasons:
#
#   1. Caddy serves the whole app on 443, so requests are same-origin.
#      CORS does not apply and the browser never needs port 4000 open.
#   2. An `https://` page may not make plain-HTTP requests. A second
#      origin on port 4000 is blocked as mixed content regardless of
#      what CORS returns, so pointing the client straight at the API
#      cannot work over TLS.
#
# The `/api` prefix is stripped by whichever is the front door:
# `deploy/Caddyfile` via `handle_path /api/*`, or `next.config.ts` if you
# are going through the Next dev server. Either way the browser only ever
# sees one origin.
#
# The WebSocket follows the page's scheme instead (`getSocketUrl()`):
# same origin over `https://`, and port 4000 directly over `http://`.
# The split is forced, not a preference — Next's rewrite proxy does not
# handle the HTTP `upgrade` event, so a `ws://` request to a rewritten
# `/socket.io` path never connects.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=/api

# Where the rewrites proxy to. Read by the Next server only, so it is NOT
# inlined into the client bundle — it can stay a loopback address and does
# not need changing when the LAN IP changes.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:4000

# Where plain-HTTP visitors to port 3000 are sent. Read by the Next server
# only (see proxy.ts), NOT inlined into the client bundle.
#
# This must be the address the certificate was issued for — the same value
# as SITE_ADDRESS in ../deploy/restart-proxy.sh. Change one, change the
# other.
#
# Why it exists: chat works fine on `http://192.168.1.46:3000`, which is
# what makes the wrong URL so easy to land on and so hard to notice. But
# that origin is not secure, so the browser never exposes
# `navigator.mediaDevices` there and the call buttons are dead. Setting
# this turns the mistake into a 308 to the origin that does work, instead
# of a page that looks entirely normal and cannot call.
#
# Leave it unset for `npm run dev` and for Mode B below. The redirect does
# not exist when this is empty, and localhost is exempt regardless.
HTTPS_ORIGIN=https://192.168.1.46

# ============================================================
# MODE B — plain HTTP on a LAN address (chat only, no calling)
# ============================================================
# Leave NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL UNSET, keep the port, and leave HTTPS_ORIGIN
# unset — in this mode there is no HTTPS origin to send anyone to.
# Leave NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL UNSET and keep the port.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_PORT=4000
#
# The client then derives the API origin at runtime from the page it is
# already on (same hostname, NEXT_PUBLIC_API_PORT). That is deliberate:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined at BUILD time, so hardcoding
# http://localhost:4000 would send every visitor on the LAN to port 4000
# of their own machine. One build then works on localhost, on a LAN IP,
# and on a DNS name alike, with no rebuild when the IP changes.
#
# Set it to an absolute URL only if the API genuinely lives on another
# host or port:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.example.com
#
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_PORT=4000
